1
00:00:00,260 --> 00:00:08,240
 The United States.

2
00:00:08,400 --> 00:00:11,360
 Well, it won its independence, and now at the end of the 18th century,

3
00:00:11,440 --> 00:00:12,700
 the population was 5 million.

4
00:00:12,920 --> 00:00:15,240
 Five or six cities placed along the eastern seaboard

5
00:00:15,240 --> 00:00:16,860
 were already worthy of the name.

6
00:00:17,180 --> 00:00:19,820
 Philadelphia, of course, with 46,000 people.

7
00:00:20,100 --> 00:00:21,820
 New York with 33,000.

8
00:00:21,920 --> 00:00:24,740
 Baltimore, Boston, Charleston with just a few thousand.

9
00:00:24,940 --> 00:00:27,460
 But before long, it would grow out of all recognition.

10
00:00:27,460 --> 00:00:31,320
 And this was in part due to the men who loved wide-open spaces,

11
00:00:31,480 --> 00:00:32,980
 and one of them was Daniel Boone.

12
00:00:33,830 --> 00:00:36,030
 Even as a boy, he showed what he was made of.

13
00:00:36,570 --> 00:00:40,190
 As for you, Daniel, you're 12, and all you want to do is run about the woods.

14
00:00:40,310 --> 00:00:41,430
 You can't even write your name.

15
00:00:42,250 --> 00:00:44,010
 A goodness only knows what you'll become.

16
00:00:44,230 --> 00:00:45,370
 Go look after the animals.

17
00:00:45,450 --> 00:00:46,210
 It's all you can do.

18
00:01:05,560 --> 00:01:50,970
 Daniel Boone grew up in the forest, learning from the Indians and observing the creatures of nature.

19
00:02:01,830 --> 00:02:03,730
 I got in today.

20
00:02:04,200 --> 00:02:07,230
 Can't do many things, Daniel, but I do admit you're a hunter.

21
00:02:08,030 --> 00:02:08,800
 Wait there.

22
00:02:08,920 --> 00:02:10,630
 I've got something for you.

23
00:02:17,620 --> 00:02:19,410
 Father, it's a beauty.

24
00:02:19,410 --> 00:02:20,910
 With this, I can really hunt.

25
00:02:23,370 --> 00:02:28,010
 First planet, you really are.

26
00:02:39,340 --> 00:02:52,380
 I see you have two rifles now.

27
00:02:52,550 --> 00:02:53,530
 Yes, you're right.

28
00:02:54,510 --> 00:03:10,980
 this one's yours hunting all right you never know we might run into some little game or even some

29
00:03:10,980 --> 00:04:27,680
 big game black bear king of the forest 160 hectares of good carolina land that for the ridiculous

30
00:04:27,680 --> 00:04:32,500
 sum of 50 pounds my dear sir it's a steal i promise you i think we could settle down here

31
00:04:32,500 --> 00:04:56,470
 rebecca set up his family home he created a fine farm but that the call of the unexplored lands was

32
00:04:56,470 --> 00:05:06,400
 too great for him to resist the harvest is in and all the animals are fenced in everything is as it

33
00:05:06,400 --> 00:05:09,290
 Go and explore out there the western land.

34
00:05:09,290 --> 00:05:11,690
 Come back soon, don't worry.

35
00:05:11,690 --> 00:05:49,100
 My dear, I know I'm your step.

36
00:05:49,100 --> 00:06:02,350
 Becca, the ha--

37
00:06:02,350 --> 00:06:04,350
 I know the harvest is in the animals cared for,

38
00:06:04,350 --> 00:06:06,440
 and you want to explore the west.

39
00:06:06,440 --> 00:06:20,840
 Oh, it'll be my last expedition.

40
00:06:20,840 --> 00:06:22,840
 My dear, we have debts to pay,

41
00:06:22,840 --> 00:06:24,840
 so I'll have to bring in lots of skins,

42
00:06:24,840 --> 00:06:51,850
 but this time I won't go alone.

43
00:06:51,850 --> 00:06:56,970
 We hunt more than we need for their meat or hide.

44
00:06:56,970 --> 00:06:58,970
 Have you not killed more than you need?

45
00:06:58,970 --> 00:07:02,030
 When we hunt, we kill because it's what we like.

46
00:07:02,030 --> 00:07:04,030
 And we sell their hides. They don't belong to anyone.

47
00:07:04,030 --> 00:07:07,120
 They belong to everyone and should be respected.

48
00:07:07,120 --> 00:07:09,730
 I'll show you just how I respect 'em.

49
00:07:09,730 --> 00:07:16,340
 White man has to understand. Massacring animals is wrong way.

50
00:07:16,340 --> 00:07:20,490
 Animals belong to all people, like the sky and the clouds and the wind on the prairie.

51
00:07:20,490 --> 00:07:30,170
 Try to teach me, savage.

52
00:07:30,170 --> 00:07:37,960
 Good shot. Let's take a look. Where'd the chief go? Hey, where is it?

53
00:07:37,960 --> 00:07:43,970
 Killed more animals. I get more cash.

54
00:07:43,970 --> 00:07:48,520
 And that's right what he says. We killed more than all of you, so we better get most of the money.

55
00:07:48,520 --> 00:07:53,350
 No, I don't agree. No, we're a hunting party and we're all sh--

56
00:07:53,350 --> 00:07:56,350
 No, no, we kill them and we get our money. In fact--

57
00:07:56,350 --> 00:07:57,710
 That's right. We...

58
00:07:57,710 --> 00:08:04,730
 You Shawnees were all friends. Tell me, why do you do this, Great Chief?

59
00:08:05,080 --> 00:08:07,780
 That one killed one of my sons who did nothing to him.

60
00:08:08,220 --> 00:08:10,650
 And when you kill so many animals, it is a crime.

61
00:08:11,010 --> 00:08:12,930
 You are our friend. You can take your skins.

62
00:08:13,110 --> 00:08:14,930
 But these he killed, we will keep them.

63
00:08:22,130 --> 00:08:31,210
 Boone and the other pioneers explored many new territories, and the United States went on growing.

64
00:08:31,210 --> 00:08:36,280
 The brand new capital was established, Washington, in its own District of Columbia,

65
00:08:36,440 --> 00:08:42,610
 And the same year Vermont joined the union, then Kentucky, Tennessee.

66
00:08:42,610 --> 00:08:47,170
 But to go on expanding, there was one great obstacle: the territory of Louisiana belonged

67
00:08:47,170 --> 00:08:48,320
 to France.

68
00:08:48,320 --> 00:08:52,130
 Thomas Jefferson was president then, and he wanted to buy New Orleans.

69
00:08:52,130 --> 00:08:57,660
 Talleyrand, try to sell these Americans not just New Orleans, but the complete Louisiana

70
00:08:57,660 --> 00:08:58,660
 territory.

71
00:08:58,660 --> 00:08:59,620
 Why not?

72
00:08:59,620 --> 00:09:04,180
 After all, what good could such land ever be to us?

73
00:09:04,180 --> 00:09:09,720
 Gentlemen, all of Louisiana for $20 million.

74
00:09:09,720 --> 00:09:10,720
 What?

75
00:09:10,720 --> 00:09:12,820
 You want us to pay $20 million?

76
00:09:12,820 --> 00:09:14,940
 We don't have authority for that!

77
00:09:14,940 --> 00:09:20,350
 I've been commissioned to buy New Orleans for two million dollars, three, let's say.

78
00:09:20,350 --> 00:09:22,350
 Nineteen million, not a penny less.

79
00:09:22,350 --> 00:09:24,110
 That's much too high, let's say four.

80
00:09:24,110 --> 00:09:25,370
 Eighteen million.

81
00:09:25,370 --> 00:09:27,290
 Too high, five million.

82
00:09:27,290 --> 00:09:28,700
 Then seventeen.

83
00:09:28,700 --> 00:09:30,240
 No, six million.

84
00:09:30,240 --> 00:09:32,850
 Sixteen million dollars, my final offer.

85
00:09:32,850 --> 00:09:33,790
 No, seven.

86
00:09:33,790 --> 00:09:34,410
 Fifteen.

87
00:09:34,410 --> 00:09:35,050
 Eight.

88
00:09:35,050 --> 00:09:35,690
 Fourteen.

89
00:09:35,690 --> 00:09:36,410
 Nine.

90
00:09:36,410 --> 00:09:37,580
 Thirteen.

91
00:09:37,580 --> 00:09:38,750
 Very well, ten.

92
00:09:38,750 --> 00:09:40,310
 Twelve, but it's my final price.

93
00:09:40,310 --> 00:09:41,440
 Eleven, and that's the limit.

94
00:09:41,440 --> 00:09:43,290
 I think it's a fair offer.

95
00:09:43,290 --> 00:09:45,380
 Done.

96
00:09:45,380 --> 00:09:49,650
 Louisiana was purchased for 11 million dollars and was well worth it.

97
00:09:49,730 --> 00:09:53,330
 It worked out at 10 cents per hectare, the cost of a box of matches.

98
00:09:54,340 --> 00:09:56,000
 It was a real bargain, huh?

99
00:09:56,270 --> 00:09:58,580
 One of the best deals ever made for the Americans.

100
00:09:59,020 --> 00:10:01,800
 Overnight, the territory of the U.S. doubled in size.

101
00:10:02,120 --> 00:10:05,100
 Population soared because new immigrants came in every day.

102
00:10:05,460 --> 00:10:08,540
 The country expanded to the West, always to the West.

103
00:10:10,690 --> 00:10:18,590
 Members of the Union.

104
00:10:19,740 --> 00:10:22,230
 Louisiana, naturally.

105
00:10:22,790 --> 00:10:23,470
 Indiana.

106
00:10:24,190 --> 00:10:24,590
 Mississippi.

107
00:10:25,410 --> 00:10:25,690
 Illinois.

108
00:10:26,890 --> 00:10:27,570
 Alabama.

109
00:10:27,770 --> 00:10:38,330
 Maine, Missouri, the grand total of 24, 24 states made up the USA, but they weren't all the same, they were differences.

110
00:10:38,750 --> 00:10:40,190
 What were these differences?

111
00:10:40,570 --> 00:10:46,870
 Well, one thing, in only half of these states were men free, it was slavery in the others, in half of the states there was slavery.

112
00:10:48,540 --> 00:10:50,760
 Oh, faster, faster!

113
00:10:51,340 --> 00:10:53,340
 You sleep, you lazy bones, good for nothings.

114
00:10:53,460 --> 00:10:54,140
 Get a move on!

115
00:10:54,720 --> 00:10:57,930
 Ready not, in two baskets of cotton, gets whipped tonight!

116
00:10:58,840 --> 00:11:00,490
 I like whipping them.

117
00:11:07,000 --> 00:11:12,930
 The boy tried to run away, and we're going to give him a lesson.

118
00:11:13,050 --> 00:11:15,710
 No, don't hurt him. He's a child. He didn't know any better.

119
00:11:15,980 --> 00:11:17,960
 If he's a child, it's about time he learned.

120
00:11:18,430 --> 00:11:19,990
 No, we won't run so fast.

121
00:11:20,760 --> 00:11:22,890
 There was another big problem.

122
00:11:23,820 --> 00:11:26,490
 We have to solve our Indian problem.

123
00:11:27,440 --> 00:11:31,620
 Cherokees living in Georgia will send to the west, beyond the Mississippi River.

124
00:11:31,880 --> 00:11:32,980
 We'll sign a treaty with them.

125
00:11:33,220 --> 00:11:35,500
 They will have a reservation and live in peace here.

126
00:11:37,160 --> 00:11:41,270
 What? Black Falcon and all of his tribe have left the reservation?

127
00:11:41,430 --> 00:11:44,370
 There was no way to feed the tribe there, Mr. President.

128
00:11:44,610 --> 00:11:48,050
 Dispatch army units there to make them return.

129
00:11:48,050 --> 00:11:51,310
 I don't want any Indians east of the Mississippi.

130
00:11:51,310 --> 00:11:52,220
 And the Shawnee?

131
00:11:52,220 --> 00:12:10,670
 They're migrating, Mr. President. They're migrating.

132
00:12:10,670 --> 00:12:13,670
 350 treaties were signed with the Indians,

133
00:12:13,670 --> 00:12:16,860
 and almost none was ever honored by the Americans.

134
00:12:16,860 --> 00:12:21,170
 Thousands and thousands of Native Americans lost their lives

135
00:12:21,170 --> 00:12:23,170
 in forced migrations to the West.

136
00:12:23,170 --> 00:12:30,240
 Arkansas joined the Union, expanding beyond Louisiana,

137
00:12:30,240 --> 00:12:33,240
 and the United States encroached on Mexican territory.

138
00:12:33,240 --> 00:12:35,240
 The situation there was changeable.

139
00:12:35,240 --> 00:12:52,150
 That coming. Must be the new governor.

140
00:12:52,150 --> 00:13:01,120
 The old one is leaving.

141
00:13:01,120 --> 00:13:03,120
 Senor, slavery is not permitted in Mexico.

142
00:13:03,120 --> 00:13:05,240
 Senor, now I must ask you to release them.

143
00:13:05,240 --> 00:13:07,240
 I got authority to work in Mexico,

144
00:13:07,240 --> 00:13:09,240
 according to the laws we got at home.

145
00:13:09,240 --> 00:13:11,520
 Our president, General Santa Ana,

146
00:13:11,520 --> 00:13:13,520
 would not like hearing that. He won't be happy.

147
00:13:13,520 --> 00:13:15,520
 You sent for me, General Santa Ana.

148
00:13:15,520 --> 00:13:19,060
 It is scandalous what is going on in Texas.

149
00:13:19,060 --> 00:13:22,210
 I won't permit it. Every report says the same thing.

150
00:13:22,210 --> 00:13:24,160
 The North Americanos do whatever they like.

151
00:13:24,160 --> 00:13:28,030
 General Cus, take a detachment and put things right in the territory of Texas.

152
00:13:28,030 --> 00:13:30,210
 Yes, General. Uh, Presidente.

153
00:13:30,210 --> 00:13:40,320
 For the Americanos, there they are.

154
00:13:40,320 --> 00:13:41,760
 They aren't many, we're lucky.

155
00:13:41,760 --> 00:13:44,680
 And we will advance and with our fine army we massacre them.

156
00:13:58,960 --> 00:14:00,400
 I'm in charge around here.

157
00:14:00,400 --> 00:14:04,400
 Now, you can all go on home as long as you agree you'll behave yourselves.

158
00:14:04,400 --> 00:14:05,840
 Come on now, promise.

159
00:14:05,840 --> 00:14:07,120
 I'll give you my word of honor.

160
00:14:08,830 --> 00:14:11,700
 My word of honor, my heart.

161
00:14:11,700 --> 00:14:14,430
 You simply no good rapscallion.

162
00:14:14,430 --> 00:14:16,770
 Caramba, you yellow belly.

163
00:14:16,770 --> 00:14:19,020
 Now I'll show these gringos who I am.

164
00:14:19,470 --> 00:14:22,370
 They're not going to insult President General Santa Anna, are they?

165
00:14:22,640 --> 00:14:23,580
 No, they're not!

166
00:14:34,290 --> 00:14:37,590
 Colonel Bowie, the Mexican army's coming.

167
00:14:37,690 --> 00:14:38,590
 There must be thousands.

168
00:14:38,990 --> 00:14:41,790
 Colonel, tell me exactly how many men do we have?

169
00:14:41,870 --> 00:14:43,590
 I'll tell you, Davy Crockett, about 200.

170
00:14:44,100 --> 00:14:45,630
 Well, we're at your orders, Colonel Travis.

171
00:14:45,770 --> 00:14:46,810
 Thank you, Colonel Bowie.

172
00:14:46,950 --> 00:14:48,470
 Take the women and children away.

173
00:15:08,080 --> 00:15:31,710
 Less than 200 of them, you can go home.

174
00:15:33,790 --> 00:15:35,900
 If you want Fort Almond, take it.

175
00:15:35,900 --> 00:16:07,820
 It's held out for 1,500 Mexicans paid with their lives at the Alamo.

176
00:16:08,220 --> 00:16:11,140
 There were only five wounded men, and they were killed.

177
00:16:11,740 --> 00:16:14,770
 A few years later, the United States declared war on Mexico.

178
00:16:15,100 --> 00:16:18,980
 Allowing in Cortez's footsteps, General Scott would conquer Mexico,

179
00:16:18,980 --> 00:16:23,320
 and the United States would acquire Texas, New Mexico, and California.

180
00:16:23,900 --> 00:16:30,900
 Mexico, Utah, California joined the Union.

181
00:16:31,220 --> 00:16:34,200
 Only one region remained unexplored, the Oregon Territory.

182
00:16:34,280 --> 00:16:37,120
 But before long, pioneers began streaming in there, too.

183
00:16:37,180 --> 00:16:41,050
 St. Louis on the Mississippi, all ashore that's going ashore.

184
00:16:43,360 --> 00:16:46,440
 Let me, sir, find the Oregon Trail.

185
00:16:46,990 --> 00:16:48,850
 Oh, you too, huh?

186
00:16:49,740 --> 00:16:55,020
 Go up the Missouri River, 250 miles up, to the City of Independence.

187
00:16:55,440 --> 00:16:56,440
 The trail starts right there.

188
00:16:57,030 --> 00:16:58,990
 Then it's only about 2,000 miles to go.

189
00:16:59,290 --> 00:17:00,570
 Good luck. Hope you make it.

190
00:17:13,070 --> 00:17:35,930
 For a carpenter, can handle livestock, has to know how to cook and chop wood.

191
00:17:36,060 --> 00:17:37,490
 We leave for the Oregon Trail tomorrow morning.

192
00:17:37,490 --> 00:17:39,270
 I'll go. I'll go with you, sir.

193
00:17:39,370 --> 00:17:43,370
 I don't know if I'm too good at all those things yet, but I'm strong and willing to learn.

194
00:17:43,370 --> 00:17:45,250
 No, it's me you want. I'll go with you.

195
00:17:47,050 --> 00:17:50,020
 Handle a six-gun, you never know.

196
00:17:50,370 --> 00:17:53,480
 I might want an engine to bite the dust or have some troublesome neighbors.

197
00:17:53,980 --> 00:18:00,350
 It's our destiny to go west, spreading across the continent.

198
00:18:00,470 --> 00:18:01,590
 We need peace in our hearts.

199
00:18:02,490 --> 00:18:06,350
 I have no intention of killing Indians and even less of shooting my neighbors.

200
00:18:06,570 --> 00:18:08,650
 I like your honesty, young man. What's your name?

201
00:18:09,340 --> 00:18:10,950
 Peter. So am I coming along?

202
00:18:11,300 --> 00:18:12,400
 You are, Peter. You're hired.

203
00:18:12,520 --> 00:18:13,780
 By the way, my name's Williams.

204
00:18:14,260 --> 00:18:15,570
 Come on, I'll show you the wagon.

205
00:18:19,840 --> 00:18:23,910
 Friends, who is voting for me to be wagon master?

206
00:18:23,990 --> 00:18:27,130
 I'm the best man for the job, and my assistant is an excellent shot.

207
00:18:27,570 --> 00:18:28,730
 We vote for the Williams!

208
00:18:38,310 --> 00:18:38,880
 Westward home!

209
00:20:20,030 --> 00:20:20,430
 Snowstorm.

210
00:20:20,430 --> 00:20:23,180
 Find shelter. We could freeze in this blizzard.

211
00:20:39,700 --> 00:20:41,620
 Why didn't you help to swing my election?

212
00:20:41,880 --> 00:20:43,040
 What could I do?

213
00:20:43,160 --> 00:20:44,360
 Make them an offer they couldn't...

214
00:20:44,360 --> 00:20:57,960
 Sugar.

215
00:20:58,840 --> 00:21:00,470
 Maybe a couple of breakfast rolls?

216
00:21:00,530 --> 00:21:01,490
 And slices of bacon.

217
00:21:01,690 --> 00:21:03,190
 How come you've no provisions, man?

218
00:21:03,270 --> 00:21:05,430
 No tools with you either? That's bad planning.

219
00:21:06,370 --> 00:21:09,600
 Oh, we don't have tools for working. We're property developers.

220
00:21:10,920 --> 00:21:12,950
 Tell me again what you developed.

221
00:21:13,030 --> 00:21:14,990
 It's something new. We just got into it, Ralph.

222
00:21:15,070 --> 00:21:17,010
 We're property developers, you understand?

223
00:21:17,170 --> 00:21:18,870
 A lot of folks want land and...

224
00:21:18,870 --> 00:21:19,710
 We sell it.

225
00:21:20,110 --> 00:21:21,550
 Yeah, but then we get more.

226
00:21:21,550 --> 00:21:27,290
 And we sell, we buy 'em, and we sell 'em, and buy and sell, buy and sell.

227
00:21:27,290 --> 00:21:32,820
 Well, we raise pigs and we make pork and bacon, we sell grain and harvest and we make our own bread.

228
00:21:32,820 --> 00:22:31,880
 What you're describing doesn't seem to be of much use, especially since land is free out here.

229
00:22:31,880 --> 00:22:35,880
 Cannot put the man into your hands, he'll be judged, and we'll give you blankets, axes, and a mare.

230
00:22:35,880 --> 00:22:42,050
 White man will not buy a brave's life since you refuse. We will do justice.

231
00:22:42,050 --> 00:23:42,840
 Build our house on this province. We'll plant foot of those hills over there.

232
00:23:42,840 --> 00:23:44,420
 we will build your house.

233
00:23:44,420 --> 00:23:47,780
 There's certainly no shortage of space.

234
00:23:47,780 --> 00:23:50,860
 Let's get to work.

235
00:23:50,860 --> 00:23:54,120
 Pioneers found themselves 2,000 miles

236
00:23:54,120 --> 00:23:56,540
 from what they would have called civilization.

237
00:23:56,540 --> 00:24:02,580
 Life was hard and dangerous.

238
00:24:02,580 --> 00:24:05,830
 Yet the farms and hamlets were to become

239
00:24:05,830 --> 00:24:07,490
 the cities of the future.

240
00:24:07,490 --> 00:24:12,990
 350,000 pioneers would take the Oregon Trail in the 1850s.

241
00:24:12,990 --> 00:24:16,230
 The Mormons would go to Great Salt Lake,

242
00:24:16,230 --> 00:24:20,690
 still others all the way to California.

243
00:24:20,690 --> 00:24:23,100
 One day in Sacramento, California,

